<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lelang Commandery</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">108 BCE–<a href="/years/313">313 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lelang was a Chinese commandery set up by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> in 108 BCE after its conquest of Gojoseon, with its centre near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a>. For four centuries it was a channel for Chinese culture, writing and trade into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and its tombs have yielded fine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lacquerware">lacquerware</a> and other luxury goods. Goguryeo conquered it in 313.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lelang-commandery.jpg" alt="Lelang Commandery" width="320" height="501" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Asieon · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Early_4th_century_Lelang_Commandery.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 108 BCE Emperor Wu of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> conquered the realm of King Ugŏ, a grandson of King Wiman, and set up four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> peninsula: Lelang, Lintun, Xuantu and Zhenfan. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-han">Book of Han</a> records Lelang, in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/youzhou">Youzhou</a>, as 25 prefectures with 62,812 households and 406,748 people. Its capital, the prefecture of Joseon, lay at modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a>, where the district of Rakrang still bears its name. After Emperor Wu's death Zhenfan and Lintun were abolished and some of their prefectures joined Lelang, which was then divided under defenders of its eastern and southern sections.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Wang clan, said to have fled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> around 180 BCE, became prominent in Lelang. In 25 CE Wang Tiao, born in the commandery, seized power and killed the governor, until Wang Zun, appointed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-guangwu-of-han">Emperor Guangwu</a>, ended the rising in 30 CE. Later, for want of men, the seven eastern prefectures were given up to the chiefs of the Eastern Ye, who were made marquises. In 238 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-yi">Sima Yi</a>, on the orders of Emperor Ming of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a>, overthrew the Gongsun family and annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a>, Lelang and Daifang, and sent home settlers who wished to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The Book of Jin counts only 8,600 households in Lelang and Daifang.</p><p>Civil wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> cut off the Jin dynasty's officials early in the 4th century, and over a thousand households submitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-hui">Murong Hui</a>, who moved them into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a>. In 313 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/micheon-of-goguryeo">Micheon of Goguryeo</a> annexed the territory. Goguryeo kept much of its administration, using refugees from China such as Dong Shou, buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anak-tomb-no-3">Anak Tomb No. 3</a>, and in 334 built the fortress of Pyongyang-song in the former commandery.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Japanese archaeologists of the colonial era found a walled site on the south bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taedong-river">Taedong River</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a> with seals and roof tiles bearing the name Lelang; most of the finds are shown in the Nangnang Gallery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a>. Korean resistance historians such as Sin Ch'aeho placed Lelang in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> instead, and North Korean scholars since 1948 have held that the Han ruled only a Lelang on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-river">Liao River</a>. Andrew Logie notes that contemporary sources and modern academic consensus place its seat at Pyongyang, and wooden <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> tablets found there in the early 1990s point the same way.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lelang Commandery” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Expansion resumed with the capture of <b>Lelang</b> in 313 and Daifang soon after, ending four centuries of Chinese commandery rule in Korea, although King Gogugwon was killed defending Pyongyang against Baekje in 371.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its iron was its great asset: Byeonhan supplied iron to Mahan, Wa in Japan and the <b>Lelang</b> and Daifang commanderies, and iron ingots served as currency.</blockquote><a href="/state/byeonhan">Byeonhan</a></li><li><blockquote>After 108 BCE it came under the Han commandery of Lintun, later folded into <b>Lelang</b>, and Chinese sources treat its people as kin of Goguryeo in language and custom.</blockquote><a href="/state/dongye">Dongye</a></li><li><blockquote>Kings at Gimhae controlled iron production and a harbour where traders from <b>Lelang</b>, Daifang and Wa called.</blockquote><a href="/state/geumgwan-gaya">Geumgwan Gaya</a></li><li><blockquote>Han set up four commanderies, including <b>Lelang</b>, and later Korean states looked back to Gojoseon as the founding Korean polity.</blockquote><a href="/state/gojoseon">Gojoseon</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Gongsun Kang created Daifang Commandery south of <b>Lelang</b> around 204 and in 207 sent Cao Cao the heads of the fugitive Yuan brothers, buying recognition.</blockquote><a href="/state/gongsun-yan">Gongsun Yan</a></li></ul></section>
